G e n e r a l   A l g e b r a i c   M o d e l i n g   S y s t e m
C o m p i l a t i o n


      CEE 6410 Fall 2015
      Example 6 in the Lecture notes for 9/29
       
      Minimize cost to supply water by a new treatment plant or wholesale contract
      This is the relaxed version that treats the interger decision variables
      as standard, positive, continuous variables.
      With the additional $35,000 capital cost for establishing the wholesale contract.
       
      Integer version using INTEGER VARIABLES statement and SOLVE as MIP.
       
      Code by 
      David E Rosenberg
      david.rosenberg@usu.edu
      September 28, 2015
       
      Run by 
      Talha Quddoos
      talha.quddoos@usu.edu
      October 09, 2026
       
       
  24   
  25  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  26  SETS src water supply sources /tp "treatment plant", wc "wholesale contract"/;
  27   
  28  * 2. DEFINE input data
  29  PARAMETERS
  30     CapCost(src) capital cost ($ to build)
  31           /tp 90000,
  32            wc 35000/
  33     OpCost(src) operating cost ($ per ac-ft)
  34           /tp 120
  35            wc 150/
  36     MaxCapacity(src) Maximum capacity of source when built (ac-ft per year)
  37            /tp 2000,
  38             wc  12000/
  39     MinUse(src) Minimum required use of source when built (ac-ft per year)
  40            /tp 0,
  41             wc  1000/
  42     TotDemand  Total Demand (ac-ft per year) /2000/
  43  * "Integer" variables free within 0 to 1 bounds. Same as "Binary Variables" statement below
  44  * Leave values as is
  45     IntUpBnd(src) Upper bound on integer variables (#)
  46            /tp 1,
  47             wc 1/
  48     IntLowBnd(src) Lower bound on integer variables (#)
  49             /tp 0,
  50             wc 0/
  51   
  52  * 3. DEFINE the variables
  53  VARIABLES I(src) binary decision to build or do prject from source src (1=yes 0=no)
  54            X(src) volume of water provided by source src (ac-ft per year)
  55            TCOST  total capital and operating costs of supply actions ($);
  56   
  57  BINARY VARIABLES I;
  58  * Non-negativity constraints
  59  POSITIVE VARIABLES X;
  60   
  61  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  62  EQUATIONS
  63     COST            Total Cost ($) and objective function value
  64     MaxCap(src)     Maximum capacity of source when built (ac-ft per year)
  65     MinReqUse(src)  Minimum required use of source when built (ac-ft per year)
  66     MeetDemand      Meet demand (ac-ft per year)
  67     IntUpBound(src) Upper bound on interger variables (number)
  68     IntLowBound(src) Lower bound on integer variables (number);
  69   
  70  COST..                 TCOST =E= SUM(src,CapCost(src)*I(src) + OpCost(src)*X(src));
  71  MaxCap(src) ..           X(src) =L= MaxCapacity(src)*I(src);
  72  MinReqUse(src) ..        X(src) =G= MinUse(src)*I(src);
  73  MeetDemand ..            sum(src,X(src)) =G= TotDemand;
  74  IntUpBound(src) ..       I(src) =L= IntUpBnd(src);
  75  IntLowBound(src) ..      I(src) =G= IntLowBnd(src);
  76   
  77  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
  78  MODEL WatSupplyRelaxed /ALL/;
  79   
  80  * 6. Solve the Model as an LP (relaxed IP)
  81  SOLVE WatSupplyRelaxed USING MIP MINIMIZING TCOST;
  82   
  83  DISPLAY X.L, I.L, TCOST.L;
  84   
  85  * Dump all input data and results to a GAMS gdx file
  86  Execute_Unload "Ex6-3-integer.gdx";
  87  * Dump the gdx file to an Excel workbook
  88  Execute "gdx2xls Ex6-3-integer.gdx"


